/-!
# Allocator ladder, rung 08t: textbook boundary tags (open; a limit of the loose interface)

`examples/alloc-ladder/08t-boundary-textbook/alloc.c` at `-O2` (`pinned/alloc`): Knuth's
boundary tags as in CS:APP 9.9. A 4096-byte arena (`heap`, `0x4030b0`) bracketed by an
allocated prologue footer and an allocated epilogue header; every block, free **or
allocated**, has a header and a footer word holding its size with the allocated bit (bit 0).
A free block holds its list links in its payload (`next` at `+8`, `prev` at `+16`). One doubly
linked free list (`flist`, `0x4030a0`), first fit, the tail of a fit that leaves at least 32
bytes is handed out; payloads start after the header and are 16-aligned. `free(p)` reads the
header of the next block and the footer of the previous block, **whatever their state**, and
coalesces in constant time. Rung 08 (`Ladder.Boundary`) is the same allocator with the free
blocks marked in side tables instead.

## Why the loose interface cannot state its safety

silmaril's loose interface (`Silmaril.X86.Sep.Alloc.Iface`) gives the allocator a state `mm`
and every live block a token `tok p n`, both plain separation-logic assertions: no ghost
state and no fractional permissions. An allocated block's tags must belong to one of them.

* **In `mm`** (the allocator owns every tag). Then nothing ties a token to a block that `mm`
  knows: whatever `tok` is, it can own only the client side of the block (the payload slack),
  and the same assertion is satisfied by a pointer into the middle of another block's payload
  (choose the slack inside that payload). `freeSpec` quantifies over every state satisfying
  `mm ∗ tok p n ∗ blockAny p n`, so it includes that forged one, where `free` reads a "header"
  inside the payload, i.e. memory in the frame, and may unlink through it. The spec is false
  for every choice of `tok`.
* **In the token** (each block's tags travel with it). Then `free(p)` reads the next block's
  header and the previous block's footer, which belong to *other* tokens, held by the caller
  or not at all: memory outside `free`'s precondition. On a frame where that word has bit 0
  clear, `free` unlinks through its links, writing into the frame. The spec is false again.

So `safe` for this allocator is open in a strong sense: not provable with the present logic.
What would express it is the same thing the first-fit `functional` row lacks
(`Silmaril.Libc.Shapes.Alloc`, "Functional correctness and tokens"): **ghost state** (an
authoritative map of the live blocks in `mm` and a fragment `p ↦ size` in each token, as in
Iris's `auth`), or **fractional permissions** on the tags (`mm` and the token each own half of
an allocated block's header and footer, so both can read and only together write). Rung 08
avoids the problem by never reading an allocated block's tags.

glibc's `ptmalloc` has the same shape: its chunk headers carry the size and the
`PREV_INUSE` bit of the previous chunk, and `free` reads the next chunk's header (and its
successor's `PREV_INUSE` bit) whatever its state. A safety proof of glibc's
allocator meets the same limitation.
-/

@[expose] public section

